\name{gatherNimble2}
\alias{gatherNimble2}
\title{
  Gather the second NIMBLE monitor set
}
\description{
  Internal function called by \code{\link{runNimble}} to gather the second monitor set (\code{parameters2}/\code{monitors2}) from the block dumps written by \code{\link{runNimbleBlock}}. Companion to \code{\link{gatherNimble}}, which gathers the primary set. Not exported; called via \code{manageNimble:::gatherNimble2}.

  Its kept rows are derived directly from \code{\link{gatherNimble}}'s own selection: a row is kept here if and only if its absolute NIMBLE iteration is in \code{retained.iters}, the exact set \code{gatherNimble} actually retained for the primary set (after burn-in and any \code{max.samples.saved}-driven subsample). Because \code{nt2} must be a positive integer multiple of \code{base.thin} (validated), every \code{nt2}-spaced iteration is guaranteed to also be a \code{base.thin}-spaced iteration, so this intersection is exact, not a resampling. This guarantees two properties together: the ratio of kept second-set rows to kept primary-set rows is always \code{nt2 / base.thin}, regardless of how many retry blocks a chain needed; and every kept row's \code{(chn, iter)} is an explicit, verifiable join key back to the corresponding primary-set row.

  Chains are stacked into a single matrix; chain identity is not preserved there, because this monitor set is intended for post hoc calculations over the posterior rather than for convergence diagnostics - but the returned \code{iter.key} carries chain identity (and iteration) back for any caller that needs it.

  Returns \code{NULL} with a warning if no block dump contains a second set, which is the expected result when \code{\link{runNimble}} was called without \code{parameters2}.
}
\usage{
  gatherNimble2(read.path, burnin, ni.block, nt2 = 1, base.thin,
                retained.iters, save.path = NULL)
}
\arguments{
  \item{read.path}{Path to directory containing sample blocks generated by \code{\link{runNimble}}.}
  \item{burnin}{Number of samples (if >= 1) or proportion of the chain (if < 1) to discard at the beginning of each chain.}
  \item{ni.block}{Number of samples in each sampling block.}
  \item{nt2}{Thinning rate the second monitor set was sampled at (i.e., \code{nt2} as passed to \code{\link{runNimble}}). Must be a positive integer multiple of \code{base.thin}.}
  \item{base.thin}{The primary set's thinning rate (i.e., \code{nt} as passed to \code{\link{runNimble}}) - the same value supplied to \code{\link{gatherNimble}}'s own \code{base.thin} argument for this run. Needed to validate \code{nt2} against it and to reproduce the primary set's absolute-iteration numbering.}
  \item{retained.iters}{The exact set of absolute NIMBLE iteration numbers \code{\link{gatherNimble}} retained for the primary set on this same \code{read.path}/\code{burnin}/\code{ni.block} (its own \code{retained.iters} return value). Determines exactly which second-set rows survive.}
  \item{save.path}{If provided, the returned list is also written here via \code{saveRDS}.}
}
\value{
  A list with two elements, or \code{NULL} (with a warning) if no second monitor set is found or no blocks remain after burn-in:
  \item{out}{A \code{[ndraw, nparam]} matrix with chains stacked.}
  \item{iter.key}{A \code{data.frame(chn, iter)} with one row per retained draw, aligned with \code{out}'s row order - joinable against \code{gatherNimble}'s own \code{iter.key} on \code{c("chn", "iter")} to recover the corresponding primary-set row.}
}
\author{
  Quresh S. Latif, Bird Conservancy of the Rockies.

  This function was originally written by Claude (Anthropic) in a prior session, and
  subsequently revised further with Claude's assistance during a full code review and
  in follow-up bug fixes, including deriving its selection directly from
  \code{gatherNimble}'s own retained-iteration set rather than sampling independently;
  see \code{NEWS.md} for what changed.
}
\keyword{internal}
\keyword{nimble}
\keyword{runNimble}
